# 13. DATA FRESHNESS, QUALITY AND SOURCE TRUST

Create a unified Data Reliability Engine.

Every source record must include:

```text
provider
dataset
product_version
variable
unit
spatial_resolution
observation_start
observation_end
publication_time
retrieval_time
processing_time
quality_status
quality_flags
validity_status
source_url
source_record_id
```

Build source-specific freshness policies.

Suggested starting principles:

| Source | Intended use | Freshness policy |
|---|---|---|
| IMD warnings/nowcast | Operational weather alerts | Respect official bulletin validity and update time |
| IMD forecast | Short-term agricultural planning | Respect issue time and forecast period |
| Open-Meteo | Supporting hourly/daily forecasts | Track model run and provider update time |
| GPM IMERG Early | Low-latency precipitation context | Record actual granule time and publication delay |
| SMAP L4 | Regional soil-water context | Respect actual archive publication latency |
| NASA POWER | Historical meteorological context | Track source time period and release status |
| Landsat | Periodic vegetation context | Track acquisition and processing dates |
| AGMARKNET | Daily market prices | Track arrival date and publication/retrieval time |
| Soil test | Soil profile | Track sample date and laboratory result date |
| Agricultural knowledge | Agronomic rules | Track publication/version and verification status |

If the source is unavailable, return the last valid observation with an explicit stale/cached label.

Never silently use stale information as current information.

When sources conflict, preserve the conflict and explain which source was prioritised and why.

